  <body>
    <div class="shell">
      <header>
        <a class="brand" href="/">anticipate<span>.</span></a>
        <a class="back" href="/">← BACK TO HOME</a>
      </header>

      <main>
        <section class="article-hero">
          <div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / PLAYER PROJECTION</div>

          <h1>
<h1>Tyrese Haliburton: A Career-Ending Injury? Or a <em>Career-Defining One?</em></h1>
          </h1>

          <p class="dek">
            The Achilles has long been treated as a turning point in an athlete's career.
            But what does NBA history actually tell us about what comes after?
          </p>

          <div class="meta">
            <span>NBA ANALYTICS</span>
            <span>2026–27 SEASON</span>
            <span>DATA-DRIVEN INVESTIGATION</span>
          </div>

          <figure class="hero-image">
            <img
              src="https://images.assettype.com/tribune/2025-06-23/dosvozbv/0-02-06-0c75b444bcd99a2496a22a001bbb3212dd96418968c09ed0ad3ec66215837d6c2724db3388f0a04c.jpg?w=1200&auto=format"
              alt="Tyrese Haliburton during the 2025 NBA Finals"
              fetchpriority="high"
            />
            <figcaption>
              Tyrese Haliburton's Achilles injury became one of the defining storylines of the 2025 NBA Finals.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="content">
          <article class="prose">
            <p class="opening">
              What if the Achilles heel wasn't the crippling weakness <em>the Iliad</em> made it out to be?
            </p>

            <p>
              In Greek mythology, Achilles was nearly invincible, brought down by
              the one vulnerability his legend could not erase. In basketball,
              an Achilles tendon tear can feel like a similar dividing line:
              the career before the injury, and the uncertain one that follows.
            </p>

            <p>
              Tyrese Haliburton knows that uncertainty. In Game 7 of the 2025 NBA
              Finals, he tore his right Achilles tendon. Indiana lost its floor
              general for the entire 2025–26 season, and one of the league's most
              creative passers faced a question no highlight reel could answer:
              what would he be when he returned?
            </p>

            <p>
              The question is not simply whether Haliburton can play again.
              It is whether the player who returns can reproduce the production,
              movement, and influence that made him one of the NBA's elite
              playmakers.
            </p>

            <h2>Before the injury</h2>

            <p>
              Haliburton's 2024–25 regular season provides the baseline for
              understanding what he is trying to regain. He averaged 18.6 points
              and 9.2 assists per game, while shooting 38.8% from three-point
              range. Those numbers describe more than a scorer. They describe a
              guard whose value depends on creating efficient opportunities for
              himself and for teammates.
            </p>

            <div class="stat-grid">
              <div class="stat">
                <strong>18.6</strong>
                <span>POINTS PER GAME<br />2024–25</span>
              </div>
              <div class="stat">
                <strong>9.2</strong>
                <span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br />2024–25</span>
              </div>
              <div class="stat">
                <strong>38.8%</strong>
                <span>THREE-POINT SHOOTING<br />2024–25</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              These are the benchmarks, not the prediction. A meaningful
              projection must consider the possibility of fewer minutes,
              changes in efficiency, a gradual return to workload, and the
              difference between being medically cleared and performing at
              a previous level.
            </p>

            <h2>What can NBA history tell us?</h2>

            <p>
              A single comeback story cannot tell us what Haliburton's future
              will look like. To look for a more useful answer, we need to
              examine NBA players who returned after Achilles tendon injuries
              and measure how their performance changed.
            </p>

            <p>
              The comparison needs to go beyond points per game. Minutes,
              games played, scoring efficiency, assists, rebounds and
              availability can tell different stories. A player may score less
              because he plays fewer minutes, for example, rather than because
              his performance per minute has changed.
            </p>

            <div class="callout">
              <p>
                <strong>THE CENTRAL QUESTION</strong><br />
                How much of a player's pre-injury production tends to return,
                and how closely does that historical pattern fit a guard whose
                game depends on playmaking and shooting?
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>From historical evidence to a projection</h2>

            <p>
              The next step is to turn the historical sample into a transparent
              projection for Haliburton. The comparison group, seasons included,
              minimum playing-time rules, and treatment of incomplete return
              seasons all need to be documented before interpreting the result.
            </p>

            <p>
              The model should estimate more than one possible outcome. A
              conservative scenario, a central estimate, and an optimistic
              scenario would show how sensitive the forecast is to assumptions
              about playing time and recovery.
            </p>

            <div class="method">
              <span class="status">RESEARCH NOTE</span>
              <h3>The projection is not the conclusion yet.</h3>
              <p>
                Historical comparisons and model results need to be verified
                before numerical forecasts are published. This article will
                report the sample, assumptions, validation error, and
                limitations alongside the final projection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>A comeback is not a guarantee</h2>

            <p>
              The purpose of this analysis is not to declare Haliburton
              finished, nor to promise that he will return unchanged. Historical
              data can help us estimate plausible outcomes, but it cannot
              account for every detail of an individual athlete's recovery,
              role, or future circumstances.
            </p>

            <p>
              The more useful question is not whether the Achilles is a career
              ending injury in every case. It is what the evidence says about
              the range of careers that can follow it, and where Haliburton
              might fall within that range.
            </p>

            <p>
              Because in basketball, as in mythology, the weakness that defines
              a story is not always the ending.
            </p>
          </article>

          <aside class="sidebar">
            <div class="side-card">
              <div class="eyebrow">THE QUESTION</div>
              <p>What can past NBA Achilles recoveries tell us about Haliburton's next season?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="side-card">
              <div class="eyebrow">WHAT WE MEASURE</div>
              <p>Scoring, assists, minutes, efficiency, availability, and changes from pre-injury production.</p>
            </div>

